dnlive - "DECnet live", a traffic display for exhibitions
==========================================================

A full-screen page in the style of an amber VT terminal that shows what
happens on the exhibition network, once a second:

- two graphs, the last 3 minutes in (up) and out (down): eth0, the LAN
  with the old machines, and wlan0, the uplink to RetroStar
- frames per second on eth0 by protocol: DECnet, LAT, MOP, IP, other
- "$ SHOW KNOWN NODES": the DECnet nodes heard (their hellos), with
  names from the file nodes, and the LAT services announced
- events: nodes coming and going, DECnet logical links opened and closed

Made for a Raspberry Pi 3 in a pi-top 1 (1366x768 screen) with
Raspberry Pi OS Bookworm (desktop), and nothing installed on top:
Python 3 standard library, Chromium, curl.

Install (in the unpacked folder):

  sudo ./install.sh                     eth0 = LAN, wlan0 = uplink
  sudo ./install.sh --lan eth1 --wan wlan0

That installs /opt/dnlive, the service dnlive.service (starts at boot,
page on http://127.0.0.1:8023/ only) and a desktop autostart that opens
the page full screen after login (kiosk.sh). It also switches screen
blanking off. Remove it with  sudo ./install.sh --remove

Operation:
  systemctl status dnlive               is the service running?
  journalctl -u dnlive                  its messages
  Alt+F4                                ends the full-screen browser
                                        (kiosk.sh opens it again after
                                        5 s; log out to stop it)

The service runs as its own unprivileged user with only CAP_NET_RAW
(to read frames). It puts the LAN interface into promiscuous mode, or
the card would not even take the DECnet hellos (multicast); a bridge
port is in that mode anyway. Nothing is sent, nothing is stored.

To change the node names, edit /opt/dnlive/nodes (NAME ADDRESS per
line) and run  sudo systemctl restart dnlive

